1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,400
 Having talked about stainless steel,

2
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,600
 we've now met all of the ions and seals

3
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:05,280
 that we're going to in this course.

4
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:06,880
 And while those materials,

5
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:09,560
 while ions and steels are probably the first ones

6
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:12,520
 you're going to turn to in an engineering design problem

7
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:15,960
 for reasons of availability and ubiquity and economy,

8
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:17,720
 they're not all of the metals in the world.

9
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:19,920
 And there are some engineering design problems

10
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:22,800
 that mandate the use of non-ferrous metals.

11
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:23,840
 In the same way that last week,

12
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:26,560
 we motivated our study of non-mechanical material properties

13
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:29,600
 by saying that these properties often place constraints

14
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:32,640
 on the problem that mean that the obvious solution

15
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:34,920
 in terms of a mechanical property wasn't necessarily

16
00:00:34,920 --> 00:00:37,200
 the right solution when you take all of these factors

17
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:38,240
 into account.

18
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:40,120
 Here, you'll often find yourself turning

19
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:42,320
 to a non-ferrous metal when the problem often

20
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:45,080
 has an element that involves not just how much strength

21
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:48,000
 can I get per dollar or how easily can I duck down

22
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,120
 to one steel and buy a standard section

23
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:52,280
 that has the mechanical properties I'm after.

24
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:54,840
 Oftentimes the choice to use a non-ferrous metal

25
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:57,040
 in an engineering design problem is motivated

26
00:00:57,040 --> 00:00:58,800
 by non-mechanical properties

27
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:01,120
 or other constraints that are placed on the problem.

28
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:02,920
 Perhaps you can't get away with using a steel

29
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:04,440
 because you'd like something that has a higher

30
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:07,600
 electrical conductivity or a higher thermal conductivity

31
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:09,040
 or a higher melting temperature

32
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:10,960
 or what needs to live inside of the human body

33
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:13,520
 or some other really nasty corrosive environment.

34
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:14,800
 Over this in the next few videos,

35
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:17,360
 we're going to meet a handful of non-ferrous metals.

36
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:18,200
 Like the fairest ones,

37
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:20,280
 we're not going to talk about every single metal in the world.

38
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:22,000
 We're talking about a subset of these

39
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:23,240
 so that you can take that knowledge

40
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:24,960
 and apply it to all of the metals

41
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:27,760
 you're not going to meet in this course or in materials too.

42
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:29,600
 Our first family of non-ferrous metals

43
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:31,160
 are aluminium alloys.

44
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:33,280
 Probably the very next thing you'll turn to

45
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:36,200
 after a steel to solve an engineering design problem.

46
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:38,400
 Aluminium alloys or aluminum alloys,

47
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:40,880
 if you find yourself on the other side of the world,

48
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:44,280
 as the name suggests, primarily composed of aluminium.

49
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:45,560
 These alloys are generally known

50
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:48,640
 for being quite good conductors and for being non-magnetic.

51
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:50,040
 And they're also generally known

52
00:01:50,040 --> 00:01:52,360
 for having material properties, mechanical properties,

53
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:54,800
 density and stiffness and strength

54
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:57,000
 about one third of mild steel.

55
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,400
 That being said, aluminium is a probably best known

56
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:00,800
 for having passivity.

57
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:04,080
 Like many of the other passivating metals we've talked about so far,

58
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:06,520
 aluminiums have a self-healing oxide layer

59
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:09,360
 that seals and protects them from further corrosion.

60
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:11,320
 Production of aluminium is quite complicated

61
00:02:11,320 --> 00:02:12,840
 and very energy intensive.

62
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:14,320
 It begins with a mining of boxite,

63
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:16,040
 which is a clay-like soil.

64
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:17,400
 And after some refinement,

65
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:21,040
 alumina or aluminium oxide is extracted from the boxite.

66
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:22,920
 This alumina gets filtered and heated

67
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:25,360
 and eventually smelted using electrolysis

68
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:26,720
 to form aluminium.

69
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:28,960
 This smelting process is quite energy intensive

70
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:31,560
 and is one of the reasons why recycling aluminium

71
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:34,480
 is so much more economical than making new aluminium.

72
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:36,600
 And the smelted aluminium is then typically used

73
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:38,120
 in one of two applications.

74
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:41,000
 A direct casting and the family of those aluminiums

75
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,400
 are called, unsurprisingly, casting aluminiums

76
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:46,120
 or wrought aluminiums where they're initially cast

77
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:48,000
 from the liquid state, but then further worked

78
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,720
 into sheets or extrusions or other shapes

79
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:53,120
 that you might like to build things from as an engineer.

80
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:55,200
 In a similar way to ions and steels,

81
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:57,560
 the material properties of an aluminium alloy

82
00:02:57,560 --> 00:02:59,760
 are highly dependent on the quantity

83
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:02,520
 and the types of alloying element that you include.

84
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:05,040
 And while a lot of our discussions for ions and steels

85
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:08,040
 about alloying with carbon and building up microstructures

86
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:10,720
 with phase changes don't really apply to aluminiums

87
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:13,000
 because it doesn't really alloy with carbon,

88
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:14,160
 particularly well.

89
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:15,880
 There are some aspects of this initial discussion

90
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:17,080
 that's still apply.

91
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:19,840
 You can't precipitation harden, aluminium alloys,

92
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:22,320
 age, aluminium alloys by holding it temperature

93
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:24,840
 and providing an impetus for all the alloying elements

94
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:26,720
 to collect together as a precipitate.

95
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:29,040
 And of course, by interstitially or substitutionally

96
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:30,320
 alloying with different elements

97
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:31,560
 and different amounts of elements,

98
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:33,880
 you can provide obstructions to these locations

99
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:36,680
 that increase the strength of an aluminium alloy.

100
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:38,120
 As I mentioned, generally speaking,

101
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:40,480
 aluminium alloys have a stiffness about one-third

102
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:42,200
 that of steel and in many cases,

103
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:44,800
 a strength one-third that of an equivalent steel.

104
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:47,120
 It turns out they also have about one-third the density,

105
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:48,160
 one-third the weight.

106
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:49,640
 On the face of it, it might seem like this is a bit

107
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:52,440
 of a zero-sum game that if all you care about is weight

108
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:55,000
 that it's equally good to go for a steel or an aluminium,

109
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:56,880
 but there are actually very good reasons

110
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:58,800
 why you might prefer one over the other

111
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:00,280
 in a design circumstance.

112
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:02,600
 For example, if your design involves some sort of volume

113
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:04,880
 constraint, if you only have a fixed amount of space

114
00:04:04,880 --> 00:04:07,480
 to put your part, then you may want to go with a steel.

115
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:09,840
 With an equivalent strength to weight ratio as an aluminium,

116
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:11,520
 you'll generally require less space

117
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:13,000
 to package a steel component.

118
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,360
 You'll deflect a steel component of equivalent size

119
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:17,840
 less under load than you wouldn't aluminium one.

120
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:19,880
 That being said, if weight is your primary concern

121
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:21,360
 and volume not so much,

122
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:22,720
 if you have the ability to spill out

123
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:24,400
 in volume one way or another,

124
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:26,320
 then you may want to go with an aluminium.

125
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:28,480
 As you'll soon talk about in civil 1100,

126
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:30,400
 bending loads are highly sensitive

127
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:32,160
 to the thickness of a material

128
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:33,960
 in the direction a load is applied.

129
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:35,320
 Think of the cross-section of a part

130
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:36,760
 is the less it tends to bend.

131
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:39,680
 And this is actually very much not a linear relationship.

132
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:42,920
 Bending stiffness actually grows with the cube of thickness.

133
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:44,960
 What this means is that if you use a lighter material,

134
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:47,080
 a less dense material like aluminium,

135
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:48,880
 for the same weight in your design,

136
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:50,520
 you can actually have a lot more of it.

137
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:52,920
 You can actually make a much thicker cross-section.

138
00:04:52,920 --> 00:04:55,040
 And because stiffness grows with thickness cubed

139
00:04:55,040 --> 00:04:57,440
 and weight only grows linearly with thickness,

140
00:04:57,440 --> 00:04:59,240
 this means that generally you can have a much,

141
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:01,720
 much stiffer product if you make it out of aluminium

142
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:03,600
 because you can fit more aluminium in there

143
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:04,600
 for the same weight.

144
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:07,120
 As you can imagine, the intersection of all of these variables,

145
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:09,880
 the statics, the mechanics of how the problem works

146
00:05:09,880 --> 00:05:11,760
 alongside the material properties changing

147
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:13,920
 and accounting for density and thickness

148
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:16,280
 can start to get really, really complicated.

149
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:17,920
 Thankfully, there is a way to account

150
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:20,240
 for these loading scenarios in the derivation

151
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:22,680
 of a material's index and it's something we'll be talking

152
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:24,480
 about over the next couple of weeks.

153
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:25,960
 For now, it's sufficient for us to say

154
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:27,560
 that there are actually circumstances

155
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:29,840
 load-based circumstances where you may prefer

156
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:31,680
 an aluminium over a steel.

157
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:33,480
 Another important point to make in the context

158
00:05:33,480 --> 00:05:36,440
 of talking about aluminiums is with regards to fatigue.

159
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:38,640
 Unlike steels, aluminiums don't tend to have

160
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:39,680
 an endurance limit.

161
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:41,720
 In other words, they will fail by fatigue

162
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:45,320
 even for very small loadings if subjected to enough cycles.

163
00:05:45,320 --> 00:05:47,800
 In other words, fatigue loading should be front of your mind

164
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:50,560
 any time you're designing a component out of aluminium.

165
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:52,600
 It's one of the reasons why you see a lot of cracked

166
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:54,800
 aluminium car trailers on the road.

167
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:56,520
 Whoever designed them or more likely

168
00:05:56,520 --> 00:05:58,320
 just welded them together in their shed

169
00:05:58,320 --> 00:06:00,280
 hadn't thought about all of the small vibrations

170
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:02,360
 that add up when a thing is bouncing along the road

171
00:06:02,360 --> 00:06:04,880
 all of the time and very quickly those vibrations

172
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:06,520
 add up to give you a fatigue loading

173
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:08,480
 that ends up failing the part.

174
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:10,760
 As with steels, there are many, many different flavors

175
00:06:10,760 --> 00:06:12,120
 of aluminium that you can get

176
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:14,800
 and these all have slightly different material properties.

177
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:16,800
 In general, of course, your best bet is to consult

178
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:19,240
 materials database, but here in this video,

179
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:22,200
 I'm going to very quickly go over the seven most common types,

180
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:24,480
 the seven most common series you're likely to encounter

181
00:06:24,480 --> 00:06:25,520
 as an engineer.

182
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:27,120
 The seven different series of aluminiums

183
00:06:27,120 --> 00:06:29,760
 are named according to an international designation system

184
00:06:29,760 --> 00:06:32,320
 that I'll leave a link for in the video description.

185
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:34,360
 And I certainly don't expect you to know or memorize

186
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:36,720
 every single particular grade of aluminium.

187
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:38,560
 These are the kinds of things that you see once

188
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:40,280
 and you remember the general gist over

189
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:42,280
 and then you go back and you look it up later

190
00:06:42,280 --> 00:06:43,680
 when you need the information.

191
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:45,120
 Though I will admit, it is quite helpful

192
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:46,280
 to have a general understanding

193
00:06:46,280 --> 00:06:48,560
 that this particular series of aluminium,

194
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:51,400
 X000 series of aluminium has these general properties,

195
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:53,280
 especially when you're having a conversation

196
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:55,840
 with a tradesperson who's manufacturing your part, for example.

197
00:06:55,840 --> 00:06:57,880
 Really quickly walking through these series,

198
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:01,480
 1,000 series aluminiums are mostly just pure aluminium,

199
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:03,840
 usually a purity above 99%.

200
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:06,400
 They have very little to any additional alloying elements

201
00:07:06,400 --> 00:07:08,640
 and for that reason, our excellent thermal

202
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:10,480
 and electrical conductors and are usually used

203
00:07:10,480 --> 00:07:13,640
 for this purpose and they have incredible ductility as well.

204
00:07:13,640 --> 00:07:15,640
 Obviously, having very few alloying elements

205
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:17,800
 that don't put up much of a fight to these locations

206
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:21,640
 and for this reason, they also tend to have reasonably low strengths.

207
00:07:21,640 --> 00:07:24,000
 1,000 series aluminiums are typically used in places

208
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,200
 where really high workability is required,

209
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:28,320
 like armature wire or in places where you need

210
00:07:28,320 --> 00:07:31,080
 excellent electrical conductivity like transmission lines.

211
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:33,760
 2,000 series aluminiums are still mostly aluminium

212
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:35,320
 but they're alloyed with copper.

213
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:37,240
 This alloying with copper means they can be aged,

214
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:39,400
 precipitation hardened to have yield strengths

215
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:41,760
 above that even of low carbon steels.

216
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:43,360
 Of course, the introduced copper by virtue

217
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:45,080
 of providing a block of for dislocations

218
00:07:45,120 --> 00:07:48,200
 and increasing strength also reduces ductility.

219
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:50,360
 These aluminiums have a tendency to crack when weld

220
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:52,360
 and we'll talk about some of the mechanisms for that

221
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:54,040
 when we talk about welding next week

222
00:07:54,040 --> 00:07:56,520
 but you should be aware that 2,000 series aluminiums

223
00:07:56,520 --> 00:07:58,960
 generally are not considered to be particularly weldable.

224
00:07:58,960 --> 00:08:00,920
 These 2,000 series alloys were quite common

225
00:08:00,920 --> 00:08:02,720
 for a long time in the aerospace industry

226
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:04,760
 because of their excellent mechanical properties

227
00:08:04,760 --> 00:08:06,800
 and relative ease by which you can machine them.

228
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:09,280
 In fact, one particular 2,000 series aluminium,

229
00:08:09,280 --> 00:08:13,400
 2,0001 aluminium is actually called free machining aluminium.

230
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:14,840
 It's called this because it has a tendency

231
00:08:14,840 --> 00:08:16,600
 to form small chips when it's machined

232
00:08:16,600 --> 00:08:19,520
 and these chips have a tendency to draw heat away

233
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:20,560
 from the job.

234
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:22,760
 There are other variants of 2,000 series aluminium

235
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:24,080
 that are easier to weld

236
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:26,160
 but generally speaking, if you're looking for weldability

237
00:08:26,160 --> 00:08:28,640
 at 2,000 series aluminium is probably not for you.

238
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:31,200
 3,000 series aluminiums are particularly noteworthy.

239
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:32,880
 They have a little bit of manganese in them.

240
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:34,240
 They generally have pretty high strength

241
00:08:34,240 --> 00:08:36,640
 as well as good corrosion resistance and ductility

242
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:39,200
 and they're often used in things like heat exchangers.

243
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:41,720
 4,000 series aluminiums have a lot of silicon in them.

244
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:44,000
 The purpose of which is to reduce the melting temperature

245
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:47,680
 and increase their fluidity when it's in its kind of liquid state.

246
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:49,880
 These 4,000 series aluminiums that you can buy

247
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:52,760
 more closely resemble the kind of casting aluminiums

248
00:08:52,760 --> 00:08:53,960
 that you might produce.

249
00:08:53,960 --> 00:08:55,520
 And for this reason, you'll quite often see them

250
00:08:55,520 --> 00:08:57,680
 in welding filler rods, for example.

251
00:08:57,680 --> 00:09:00,320
 5,000 series aluminiums have a little bit of magnesium

252
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:02,960
 in them which increases their strength and formability.

253
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:04,760
 They generally have pretty good corrosion resistance

254
00:09:04,760 --> 00:09:06,400
 and can be quite happily welded.

255
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:09,000
 For these reasons, you'll usually see 5,000 series aluminiums

256
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:10,480
 using places where you need something

257
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:12,320
 that's both structural and light.

258
00:09:12,320 --> 00:09:14,360
 For example, on a boat or a car-body panel

259
00:09:14,360 --> 00:09:15,560
 or as part of a bridge.

260
00:09:15,560 --> 00:09:18,320
 6,000 series aluminiums are also alloyed with magnesium

261
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:20,600
 but also tend to have a little bit of silicon in them.

262
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:23,360
 You'll probably quite often lean on a 6,000 series aluminium

263
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:24,800
 for general purpose fabrication

264
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:26,600
 because they can be readily formed

